{"t": 200.48, "repo": "python-humanize/humanize", "kind": "thinking", "text": "This explains it - run_command must be executing commands directly without shell interpretation, so Windows builtins like echo and rm aren't found since they're not standalone executables. That's why python.exe works directly but something like python -c with quoted arguments fails, since there's no shell to handle the quoting.\n\nSince quotes aren't being stripped, the quote characters get passed literally into the argument, which is why Python's parser sees a stray quote and throws a syntax error. The safer approach is to avoid inline quoted code entirely and instead write a script file and run it directly, since that already worked cleanly before - so I'll write a small script to handle the deletion instead."}
